-- TEST SCAFFOLDING ONLY — not part of the Ballast product.
--
-- Ballast never defines its own notion of an asset. The vault is written against the real
-- Canton Token Standard interface, `Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1.Holding`, so on DevNet it
-- holds whatever a real registry issued — real cBTC, cETH, USDCx.
--
-- But a Daml Script test cannot reach DevNet's registries, so *something* local has to issue
-- holdings. That is all this module is: a minimal issuer that implements the same interface.
-- The INTERFACE is real; only the issuer is local. Nothing in Ballast.Vault or Ballast.Spike
-- refers to this template — they only ever see `ContractId Holding`, which is exactly what
-- they will see against the real cBTC registry.
module Ballast.Testing.Registry where

import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1
import Splice.Api.Token.MetadataV1 (emptyMetadata)

-- Note the signatories: `admin, owner`. The instrument admin co-signs every holding it
-- issues, because it must be able to authorise transfers of it. That is not a quirk of this
-- test registry — it is inherent to how a registry administers an instrument, and it is why
-- README's threat model says the registry sees the fund's holdings of its own instrument.
template TestHolding
  with
    admin        : Party
    owner        : Party
    instrumentId : InstrumentId
    amount       : Decimal
  where
    signatory admin, owner
    ensure amount > 0.0

    interface instance Holding for TestHolding where
      view = HoldingView with
        owner
        instrumentId
        amount
        lock = None
        meta = emptyMetadata
